-- title:
--   Finiteness over intermediate fields containing a transcendental element
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $L$ be fields with $L$ a $K$-algebra, let $E$ be an intermediate field of $L/K$, and let $t \in L$ be an element of $E$ that is transcendental over $K$. Assume further that there exists some $x \in L$, transcendental over $K$, such that $L$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` generated by $x$. Then $L$ is a finite module over $E$, i.e. $[L : E] < \infty$. Thus for a field $L$ which is finite over $K(x)$ for a single transcendental $x$ — a one-variable function field in the concrete sense — every intermediate field containing any element transcendental over $K$ has finite index in $L$. The hypothesis of finiteness is stated for one particular transcendental generator $x$, while the conclusion concerns an arbitrary intermediate field containing an arbitrary transcendental $t$; no separating or finite-generation assumption on $E$ itself is imposed.
--
--   This is the standard finiteness statement for subfields of a one-variable function field: over the base $K$, any intermediate field containing a transcendental element is a field over which the whole function field is finite. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the development, where surjectivity of a map of function fields is deduced from a degree computation.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_finite_intermediateField_of_transcendental_mem.lean

